{# Worked example: a CUSTOM Jinja2 template for `cv.template`, showing what a template
   author can do beyond the packaged default (sluice/templates/cv_plain.html.j2) --
   different typography, an accent rule under each heading, and a hanging-indent bullet
   style. Point `cv.template` at a copy of this file (or your own) to use it; leave
   `cv.template` unset to keep the packaged default.

   SINGLE-COLUMN ON PURPOSE, same as the packaged default, and this is not a style
   preference -- it is load-bearing. A CV's destination is an ATS upload (`apply` stages
   it for a browser-assisted form fill), and a multi-column grid or table lays text out
   VISUALLY but extracts in the WRONG ORDER when an ATS parses the PDF back to plain
   text: column 1 top-to-bottom then column 2, or row-by-row across a table cell
   boundary, neither of which matches how a human reads the page or how the CV's own
   sections are meant to be read. Sluice cannot detect this after the fact -- a
   two-column PDF still renders without error (see the design doc's Known limitations)
   -- so the only real defence is a template author never reaching for a grid or table
   here in the first place.

   ZERO SAMPLE VALUES. Every text node below is either a CvDocument field
   (`document.name`, `.contact`, `.profile`, `.work[].company/.dates/.location/.title/
   .bullets`, `.certificates`, `.education`) or a structural heading matching
   cv/compose.py's own vocabulary (PROFILE, WORK EXPERIENCE, CERTIFICATES, EDUCATION) --
   the same two categories the packaged default is limited to, and
   tests/test_no_leaked_files.py holds this file to that bar mechanically, not just by
   convention. A worked example asked for "real CSS" is exactly the pressure that
   produces a filled-in specimen with a plausible name and employer; there is no spot
   below where a value is actually unavoidable, so none is used -- if you add one of
   your own (a printed date, a page-footer label), prefer the `Example ...` /
   `example.invalid` family over anything that could read as a real name or employer. #}
<style>
  @page { size: A4; margin: 20mm 18mm; }
  body {
    font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
    font-size: 10.5pt;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: #1a1a1a;
  }
  h1 {
    font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    font-size: 18pt;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    margin: 0 0 1mm;
  }
  .contact {
    font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    font-size: 9pt;
    color: #444444;
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    margin: 0 0 6mm;
  }
  h2 {
    font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    font-size: 10pt;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #333333;
    margin: 6mm 0 2mm;
    padding-bottom: 1mm;
    border-bottom: 1pt solid #999999;   /* the accent rule -- swap the colour, not the layout */
  }
  p { margin: 0 0 4mm; }
  .role {
    margin: 0 0 4mm;
    break-inside: avoid;   /* keep one role's bullets from splitting across a page break */
  }
  .company { font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-weight: bold; }
  .meta { font-style: italic; color: #555555; }
  ul { margin: 1.5mm 0 0; padding-left: 5mm; }
  li { margin: 0 0 1mm; padding-left: 1mm; }
</style>

<h1>{{ document.name }}</h1>
<div class="contact">{{ document.contact }}</div>

<h2>PROFILE</h2>
<p>{{ document.profile }}</p>

<h2>WORK EXPERIENCE</h2>
{% for role in document.work %}
<div class="role">
  <div class="company">{{ role.company }}</div>
  <div class="meta">{{ role.dates }} | {{ role.location }} | {{ role.title }}</div>
  <ul>{% for bullet in role.bullets %}<li>{{ bullet }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
</div>
{% endfor %}

{% if document.certificates %}
<h2>CERTIFICATES</h2>
<ul>{% for item in document.certificates %}<li>{{ item }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
{% endif %}

{% if document.education %}
<h2>EDUCATION</h2>
<ul>{% for item in document.education %}<li>{{ item }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
{% endif %}
